    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This field trip will focus on fracturing and faulting in Cretaceous carbonates rocks exposed between San Antonio and Uvalde along the Balcones Fault Zone. We will make stops in Alamo Heights, Bandera, Uvalde, and the Longhorn Quarry. We will examine structures in the Upper Cretaceous sequences including the Buda Limestone, Eagle Ford Shale, Austin Chalk, and the Anacacho Limestone. Among the structures we will see are joints and sheared joints in the Austin Chalk, pressure solution and veins assemblages in the Buda Limestone, the Eagle Ford shale being incorporated into a normal fault zone, and joints, sheared joints-based faults, and bedding-plane slip faults in the Anacacho Limestone and their impact on the distribution and flow of hydrocarbons as presently detectable in the form of asphalt. It is therefore not much an exaggeration to note that the Volcan Mine Quarry is a mirror to your shallow carbonate reservoirs as far as the concepts regarding fracture and faults and their roles in hydrocarbon storage and flow are concerned.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
